    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d935">

                <pb facs="1078" n="884" sameAs="frus:frus1945Berlinv02/pg884"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 (Potsdam)/7–3145</note>
                <head>No. 935<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">United States Delegation Working
                        Paper</hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d935-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Babelsberg</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1946-07-26">July 26, 1946</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p rendition="#center"><hi rendition="#small-caps">Memorandum</hi></p>
                <p>Forty per cent of all industrial production (manufacturing and mining) is to be
                    found in the Russian zone, although for the most part the industry is of a type
                    which consists of relatively small units, does not involve substantial amounts
                    of fixed capital and should remain in peacetime Germany (textiles, apparel,
                    paper and wood pulp, printing, ceramics and glass, saw mills, etc.). For
                    once-for-all removals the Russian Zone cannot therefore be said to contain over
                    30% of industrial equipment suitable for reparations and of the kind which the
                    Russians particularly wish.</p>
                <table cols="2" rows="11">
                    <row role="label">
                        <cell>Hence, let us say that the Russian zone has a once-for-all
                            repatriation [<hi rendition="#italic">reparation</hi>] potential
                            of</cell>
                        <cell role="num">30%</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>It is estimated that [the] Ruhr has 60% of the remaining 70%</cell>
                        <cell role="num">42%</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>Leaving in remainder of U. S., U. K. and French zones</cell>
                        <cell role="num">28%</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell role="num">100%</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>Assuming Russians entitled to receive of all once-for-all
                            reparations</cell>
                        <cell role="num">50%</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>And have available in their zone</cell>
                        <cell role="num">30%</cell>
                    </row>
                    <pb facs="1079" n="885" xml:id="pg885"/>
                    <row>
                        <cell>It remains that they should get elsewhere of the whole once-for-all
                            removals</cell>
                        <cell role="num">20%</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>As the Ruhr has 42% of the once-for-all removals in Germany, if we
                            give one-half of such removals to Russia as a <hi rendition="#italic"
                                >final settlement</hi> of all her claims, this equals</cell>
                        <cell role="num">21%</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>But the Russians would argue they got no current production from the
                            U. S., U. K. and French zones and that this reduces</cell>
                        <cell role="num">10½%</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell><p>As against the above; what I have proposed is:</p>
                            <p>Assign Russians one-half Ruhr removals, but only 50% of such
                                assignment for reparations and remaining 50% for exchange for goods
                                from their zone—</p></cell>
                        <cell/>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>Thus the amount they get in reparations is reduced by one-half from
                            the above figure and becomes</cell>
                        <cell role="num">5¼%</cell>
                    </row>
                </table>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
